<binaryimage arch="8085:LE:16:default">
<!--
    GH-6389: "8085 undocumented instructions are missing"
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/6389

    Ghidra's 8085 SLEIGH module follows the official Intel ISA and omits the
    well-known undocumented 8085 instructions.  Their opcode bytes are valid on
    real silicon but had no decode entry, so they failed to resolve a
    constructor (the decompiler aborts; in Ghidra's listing they render as `??`
    bad bytes).

    Fix (kuna GH-6389, modeled on upstream Ghidra commit 1577081): add the nine
    single-opcode undocumented instructions plus the undocumented K and V flag
    registers:
      DSUB 0x08, ARHL 0x10, RDEL 0x18, LDSI 0x38, SHLX 0xD9, LHLX 0xED,
      RSTV 0xCB, JNK 0xDD, JK 0xFD.

    Byte stream (one of each, with operands):
      0x00 08          DSUB HL,BC
      0x01 10          ARHL
      0x02 18          RDEL
      0x03 38 04       LDSI DE,(SP,0x4)
      0x05 d9          SHLX HL,(DE)
      0x06 ed          LHLX HL,(DE)
      0x07 cb          RSTV
      0x08 dd 34 12    JNK 0x1234
      0x0b fd 78 56    JK  0x5678

    Stage: S1 decode-table (SLEIGH spec-fix, no option). This testcase asserts
    every undocumented opcode now decodes to its proper mnemonic, and that no
    byte is left as an undecoded `??`.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x0">
0810183804d9edcbdd3412fd7856
</bytechunk>
</binaryimage>
